/* ==================== PEDESTRIANS & ROUTINE ACTIVITY ====================

   Harbor City is lived in. Ambient civilians walk the sidewalks and paths

   of every district on hand-laid waypoint graphs, cross roads only at

   designated crossings (waiting for a safe gap — and for the green at

   signalised junctions), pause at benches, shopfronts, the bus stop and

   other points of interest, and stop to watch when Unit 9's siren sounds

   nearby. Stationary figures give the town its routine: a customer in the

   QuickMart, a resident waiting at City Hall, workers by the loading bays,

   residents in their yards, visitors along the waterfront railing, and two

   officers walking rounds inside Precinct 9.

   Design rules that keep cases safe:

   - Mission NPCs live in MAPS[].npcs and NEVER move; ambient people are a

     separate system, are never named as witnesses, and keep their distance

     from door spots and street gates. Jay can always walk past a civilian

     (they are not solid to him) — they step aside / wait for him instead,

     so civilian activity can never block an entrance or a mission.

   - Only the current district's people are simulated or drawn, and the

     population is capped per district (at most 6 walkers + a few figures).

     People in other districts cost nothing per frame.

   - People are rebuilt deterministically (seeded by district) whenever

     Jay changes district, so a save/reload always restores the same

     dependable population — ambient state itself is not saved. */

